---
import { LAMP_DEFS } from './symbols';
import { BOX, WIDE_SLAB, placements } from './plan';

/**
 * DESIGN.md §B.11 — `/404` is "the only page that shows an empty room: the floor slab
 * and the lamp, no desks, no chair. It reuses the floor's slab symbol and its lamp
 * gradient and adds nothing, so it costs roughly zero new bytes. [...] This is the one
 * joke the site gets, and it is a joke that is also the argument."
 *
 * Everything drawn below already existed:
 *
 * - the slab is `WIDE_SLAB`, the projected outline of the same 6 x 5 room, at the same
 *   `WIDE_TRANSFORM` origin;
 * - the lamp is `#fl-lamp` and the `#fl-cone` gradient out of `LAMP_DEFS`, the string the
 *   floor's own `<defs>` are built from;
 * - the two lit shapes are the pool and the cone the floor draws at its chair station,
 *   with the chair's own `wideTransform` off `placements()` putting them over the cell
 *   the chair stands on. §B.12 already says the chair "is implied by what the cone points
 *   at, exactly as it is on /404"; this is that.
 *
 * **No geometry is authored here.** Two `d` strings are, and they are the two the floor
 * draws at the same station — the module-diamond pool and the cone between shade and
 * floor. They are the parts of the lamp that are drawn in the scene rather than in the
 * defs, because "Lights on" has to be able to reach them on `/`; nothing reaches them
 * here.
 *
 * **What is deliberately absent.**
 *
 * - **No idle loop.** §C.5's 4800ms loop animates `.fl-glow`, which is a monitor's
 *   screen. There are no monitors in an empty room, so there is no glow, no `--d` phase
 *   offset and no animation of any kind in this component — under `reduce` and without
 *   it, this renders identically.
 * - **The cone is static.** §H.3's "Lights on" is `/`'s one orchestrated moment and it
 *   belongs to the page it introduces. A 404 that performs a lighting cue is a page
 *   asking to be admired for having gone wrong.
 * - **No script, no buttons, no roster, no card slot.** Nothing here is interactive, so
 *   the whole SVG is `aria-hidden` (COPY.md §10.2's rule for the floor's scene) and the
 *   page's accessibility tree stays the headline, the link home and the plate — which is
 *   exactly what COPY.md §9 says the page is.
 *
 * **Scoping.** The `<defs>` arrive through `set:html`, so Astro cannot add its scope
 * attribute to them — the bug that rendered the whole floor as one black silhouette in
 * pass 2. Every class the injected markup carries is therefore styled `:global` below,
 * and the drawn marks use the same global class names so that one rule covers both. The
 * two fills those rules resolve from are set on this component's own scene box, exactly
 * as `.floor__scene` sets them, so both schemes follow `--tgd-room` with no second
 * palette.
 */
const chair = placements().at(-1);
if (!chair || chair.shape !== 'chair') {
  throw new Error('The floor plan no longer ends with the chair; /404 has nowhere to hang the lamp.');
}
const lamp = BOX.lamp;
const node = chair.wideNode;

/*
  The box is CROPPED to what is drawn, and it is computed rather than typed.

  §C.3's 856 x 520 scene box carries 76 units of horizontal margin for nameplates that
  overhang their module and 64 vertical for the same reason. This room has no nameplates,
  no desks and no chair, so that margin is 140 units of empty ground — at 360 it was the
  difference between a room and a smudge. The extents below are the slab's own projected
  outline, widened where the lamp's pool and the cord reach past it, and `PAD` is the
  drawing's only breathing room. Nothing here is a coordinate: change the plan and this
  follows it.
*/
const PAD = 8;
const xs = WIDE_SLAB.split(' ').map(Number).filter((_, i) => i % 2 === 0);
const ys = WIDE_SLAB.split(' ').map(Number).filter((_, i) => i % 2 === 1);
/** The pool is a full module, so it reaches a module half-width either side of the node. */
const POOL = { w: 64, h: 64 };
const minX = Math.min(...xs, node.x - POOL.w) - PAD;
const maxX = Math.max(...xs, node.x + POOL.w) + PAD;
const minY = Math.min(...ys, node.y + lamp.y) - PAD;
const maxY = Math.max(...ys, node.y + POOL.h) + PAD;
const box = { w: maxX - minX, h: maxY - minY };
---

<div class="room404" style={`aspect-ratio: ${box.w} / ${box.h}`}>
  <svg
    class="room404__scene"
    viewBox={`${minX} ${minY} ${box.w} ${box.h}`}
    aria-hidden="true"
    focusable="false"
  >
    <g set:html={`<defs>${LAMP_DEFS}</defs>`}></g>
    <polygon class="fl-slab fl-edge" points={WIDE_SLAB}></polygon>
    <g transform={chair.wideTransform}>
      <path class="fl-cone" d="M0 0 64 32 0 64-64 32Z"></path>
      <path class="fl-cone" d="M-9-56 9-56 44-4-44-4Z"></path>
      <use href="#fl-lamp" x={lamp.x} y={lamp.y} width={lamp.w} height={lamp.h}></use>
    </g>
  </svg>
</div>

<style>
  /*
    PLAN.md §4.5 and the home floor's own rule — the section reserves its height so the
    headline above it paints first and nothing shifts when it is laid out. The number is
    this room's own: the scene is 856 x 520 capped, so 520 is the tallest it renders.
  */
  /*
    The home floor's rule, and its two declarations, on the box that actually holds the
    drawing — the section skips its own layout until it is near the viewport, so the
    headline above it paints first.

    **The reservation is the `aspect-ratio`, and `contain-intrinsic-size` must stay out of
    its way.** This is the run's second finding that only a measurement produces. The home
    floor's height is content-derived and a single 1180px estimate fits it; this room's
    height is `width ÷ 5:3` and no single number fits it at more than one width. Declared
    as `auto 432px` it reserved 432px at every width against a room that renders 164px at
    360 and 190px at 412, and because `/404` is short enough that the footer is IN the
    first viewport, the whole of that jump was counted: **CLS 0.0596 against a 0.05 line,
    identical across three Lighthouse runs**, on the very page this run added. Isolated by
    re-running with `content-visibility` disabled (0) and with the fonts blocked (0.0596),
    so it was the reservation and not the swap.

    A size-contained box takes its block size from `contain-intrinsic-size` **in
    preference to** the ratio, so the fix is to give it nothing to prefer: `auto none`
    keeps the remembered size for a second paint and otherwise defers to the ratio, which
    is exact at every width, continuously, rather than at one. Measured after, at 360,
    412 and 1440: 0, 0.0027 and 0 — and the 0.0027 is the font swap, not this box.

    The ratio therefore lives on this element rather than on the <svg> inside it, which
    is also why the width cap moved up here with it.
  */
  .room404 {
    content-visibility: auto;
    contain-intrinsic-size: auto none;

    /* §C.3's cap for the drawn room — 704 units of slab plus the crop's own padding.
       The ratio beside it is set inline from the same computed box, so the reservation
       and the drawing cannot disagree. */
    max-inline-size: 720px;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  .room404__scene {
    display: block;
    inline-size: 100%;
    block-size: 100%;
  }

  /*
    These are `:global` for the same reason StudioFloor's are, and it is a bug fix rather
    than a style: the `<defs>` above are injected with `set:html`, and Astro cannot add a
    scope attribute to markup it did not compile — scoped, `.fl-cone-a[data-astro-cid-…]`
    matches nothing inside the gradient and every fill falls back to the initial black.

    They are restated here rather than borrowed from StudioFloor because this page does
    not render StudioFloor: a route must not depend on the stylesheet of a component it
    never mounts. Every value is the token the floor reads, so there is one source of
    truth for each colour and two one-line mappings to it.
  */
  :global(.fl-slab) {
    fill: url(#fl-slab-g);
  }

  :global(.fl-edge) {
    stroke: rgba(var(--f-ink-rgb), 0.16);
    stroke-width: 1;
  }

  :global(.fl-slab-a) {
    stop-color: var(--f-a);
  }

  :global(.fl-slab-b) {
    stop-color: var(--f-b);
  }

  :global(.fl-cord) {
    fill: rgba(var(--f-ink-rgb), 0.4);
  }

  :global(.fl-shade) {
    fill: var(--f-c);
    stroke: rgba(var(--lamp-rgb), 0.55);
    stroke-width: 1;
  }

  :global(.fl-cone) {
    fill: url(#fl-cone);
    opacity: 0.7;
  }

  :global(.fl-cone-a) {
    stop-color: var(--f-cone);
    stop-opacity: 0.55;
  }

  :global(.fl-cone-b) {
    stop-color: var(--f-cone);
    stop-opacity: 0.03;
  }
</style>
